{{also|Sharpness}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|sharpnesse}}, {{m|enm|scharpnesse}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sċearpnes|t=sharpness}}, equivalent to {{af|en|sharp|-ness}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} the [[cutting]] [[ability]] of an [[edge]]; [[keenness]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} the fineness of the point a pointed object.
# {{lb|en|countable}} The product or result of being [[sharp]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1950-04|author=Timothy H. Cobb|title=The Kenya-Uganda Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|page=263|text=The first thing that strikes the stranger is the '''sharpness''' of the curves on the metre gauge; it is not unusual for a long train to be travelling in three directions at once, and the engine is frequently in full view of the windows of the ninth or tenth carriage.}}
# {{lb|en|of food etc}} [[pungency]] or [[acidity]].
# {{lb|en|of an image}} [[distinctness]], [[focus]].
# {{lb|en|of intelligence}} [[acuteness]] or [[acuity]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Amusements.|magazine=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1861/06/14/archives/amusements.html|date=1861-06-14|passage=Mrs. PAGE is gifted with a pleasing voice, which is yet imperfectly weeded of certain '''sharpnesses''', but seems susceptible of a wide range of melodious inflection.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en
|date=2012-06-19
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=England 1-0 Ukraine
|work=w:BBC Sport
|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/18181971
|page=
|passage=A lack of match '''sharpness''' was perhaps to blame for Rooney squandering England's best chance after 27 minutes.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[edge]] or [[blade]]
